config VIDEO_MEDIATEK_VCODEC_VENC_MT6589
	tristate "MediaTek MT6589 H.264/VP8 video encoder (VENC)"
	depends on ARCH_MEDIATEK || COMPILE_TEST
	depends on COMMON_CLK
	depends on HAS_IOMEM
	depends on MTK_SMI || (COMPILE_TEST && MTK_SMI=n)
	help
	  Say Y here if you want to use the MediaTek MT6589 hardware video
	  encoder.

	  The MT6589 codec is an RTL accelerator with no coprocessor and no
	  firmware, unlike the later MediaTek VPU generations handled by
	  CONFIG_VIDEO_MEDIATEK_VCODEC.  The encoder is a hardwired H.264 and
	  VP8 block at 0x17002000, described in data sheet chapter 60, with
	  the MPEG-4 encoder (chapter 61) sharing the same window.

	  This driver exposes the chapter-60 H.264 datapath as a V4L2
	  memory-to-memory node: it owns the bitstream, reference,
	  reconstruction and rate control scratch buffers the address
	  registers need, programs the quantiser triple, bit rate and frame
	  rate terms the registers actually have, and retires a frame on the
	  frame-done interrupt, on a bitstream overflow, or on a watchdog when
	  the engine never signals completion at all.

	  It does not implement entropy coding, motion estimation, slice
	  control, SPS/PPS emission or the bitrate algorithm; those ship only
	  as closed-source userspace libraries with no corresponding open
	  kernel-side programming model.  Userspace must treat the encoded
	  output as opaque.  MPEG-4 encoding is refused with -ENODEV until its
	  per-MB side-information buffer is owned.

	  VENC and VDEC are separate hardware blocks and are separate drivers:
	  see CONFIG_VIDEO_MEDIATEK_VCODEC_VDEC_MT6589.  They were previously
	  one node naming two power domains, which meant neither domain was
	  attached at all; see drivers/pmdomain/mediatek/mt6589-power-domains.md.

	  To compile this driver as a module, choose M here: the module will
	  be called mtk-venc-mt6589.

config VIDEO_MEDIATEK_VCODEC_VDEC_MT6589
	tristate "MediaTek MT6589 video decoder (VDEC)"
	depends on ARCH_MEDIATEK || COMPILE_TEST
	depends on COMMON_CLK
	depends on HAS_IOMEM
	depends on MTK_SMI || (COMPILE_TEST && MTK_SMI=n)
	help
	  Say Y here if you want to use the MediaTek MT6589 hardware video
	  decoder.

	  The decoder is a multi-standard RTL block at 0x16020000 (data sheet
	  chapter 59): MPEG-1/2, MPEG-4, DivX, Xvid, H.263, VC-1, WMV9, H.264
	  up to level 4.2, VP8.  This driver provides the resource layer for
	  it -- the two vdecsys clock gates, the frame-end interrupt and its
	  acknowledgement, the VDE power domain through the standard one-domain
	  attachment -- and a V4L2 memory-to-memory node.

	  The node's device_run() reports -ENODEV, and that is deliberate.  The
	  vendor stack does not program the VDEC datapath from the kernel: its
	  kernel driver touches the decoder only to power it up and acknowledge
	  the frame-end interrupt, while the register sequence itself is
	  assembled by closed userspace libraries as a queue of WRITE_REG_CMD
	  records and shipped to the driver over the MFV_SET_CMD_CMD ioctl.
	  The decode sequence is therefore in stripped, partly encrypted blobs,
	  and the data sheet has no register-definition section for this block
	  at all.  A client can open the node, query it and negotiate formats;
	  it cannot decode.

	  To compile this driver as a module, choose M here: the module will
	  be called mtk-vdec-mt6589.

